# Normalize text handling so line endings can never corrupt published data.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# `dataset/verified_tourism_data.json` is the canonical public tourism catalog.
# Its SHA-256 is published in `verified_tourism_data.json.sha256` and in
# `verified_tourism_data.lock.json`, and CI asserts the two agree. With
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default) Git rewrites LF -> CRLF on checkout
# and CRLF -> LF on commit, which changes those bytes and silently invalidates
# the published checksum. A contributor on Windows could therefore "fix" a
# mismatch by re-committing the file and break every published download link
# while the diff looks empty.
#
# Marking the release artifacts as binary (`-text`) makes Git store and check
# out their exact bytes everywhere. Text normalization still applies to all
# source code, which is what we want.

# --- Verified data release: byte-exact, never normalized ---------------------
Tourism/dataset/verified_tourism_data.json -text
Tourism/dataset/verified_tourism_data.lock.json -text
Tourism/dataset/verified_tourism_data.schema.json -text
Tourism/dataset/verified_tourism_data.json.sha256 -text
downloads/*.sqlite3.gz -text
downloads/*.sha256 -text
downloads/*.sqlite3 -text

# --- Generated Python bytecode must never enter the repository ---------------
*.pyc binary
*.pyo binary
__pycache__/ binary

# --- Lock dependency graphs byte-exact as well -------------------------------
Tourism/requirements.txt -text
ml_service/requirements.txt -text
Tourism/requirement.txt -text

# --- Default: keep source line endings stable across platforms ---------------
* text=auto eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.sh text eol=lf
